00:34:22.840 --> 00:34:26.280
and then they would stay until two fifteen which is when the the cops

00:34:26.280 --> 00:34:28.160
throw everybody out

00:34:28.160 --> 00:34:31.560
and uh... and then they would start the long track home again

00:34:31.560 --> 00:34:36.640
now i was i was fortunate because i really did have a support structure work

00:34:36.640 --> 00:34:39.600
most prisoners don't get any visits at all

00:34:39.600 --> 00:34:42.000
or visits only very rarely

00:34:42.020 --> 00:34:43.860
and many state systems

00:34:43.860 --> 00:34:47.540
are converting to something called video visitation

00:34:47.540 --> 00:34:49.500
there's uh... there's a company

00:34:49.500 --> 00:34:51.780
called uh... jpey

00:34:51.780 --> 00:34:53.180
that run these

00:34:53.180 --> 00:34:56.500
skype like uh... video terminals

00:34:56.500 --> 00:34:59.980
uh... which sounds like a great idea you can put these video terminals in

00:34:59.980 --> 00:35:01.180
different cities

00:35:01.180 --> 00:35:04.340
and instead of making this long drive like you say a lot of people drive all

00:35:04.340 --> 00:35:04.980
night

00:35:04.980 --> 00:35:06.740
which is actually quite common

00:35:06.740 --> 00:35:09.420
uh... you can just go to a video center but the thing is that they

00:35:09.420 --> 00:35:11.460
charge you a dollar a minute

00:35:11.520 --> 00:35:12.680
the dollar a minute

00:35:12.680 --> 00:35:14.560
to have a visit

00:35:14.560 --> 00:35:18.000
who does this affect the most it affects poor people the most

00:35:18.000 --> 00:35:19.200
and that's that

00:35:19.200 --> 00:35:22.080
the company's point it's to prey on the poor

00:35:22.080 --> 00:35:24.040
so when our government says that

00:35:24.040 --> 00:35:26.160
that one of their primary objective

00:35:26.160 --> 00:35:28.000
in the in the uh...

00:35:28.000 --> 00:35:29.400
uh... prison system

00:35:29.400 --> 00:35:30.440
is to

00:35:30.440 --> 00:35:34.360
reacquaint prisoners with their families to try to help them to reintegrate

00:35:34.360 --> 00:35:36.520
with their families and to be good fathers

00:35:36.520 --> 00:35:38.280
that's just nonsense

00:35:38.280 --> 00:35:39.760
they don't mean that at all

00:35:39.760 --> 00:35:40.960
you know why

00:35:40.980 --> 00:35:43.700
these visits are a dollar a minute

00:35:43.700 --> 00:35:44.980
the bureau of prisons

00:35:44.980 --> 00:35:46.900
and the state department of corrections

00:35:46.900 --> 00:35:48.700
get a fifty percent kickback

00:35:48.700 --> 00:35:50.780
that's why they could they cost a dollar a minute

00:35:50.780 --> 00:35:52.580
anything with phone

00:35:52.580 --> 00:35:55.700
you know that you you're allowed to have in the federal system three hundred

00:35:55.700 --> 00:35:56.820
minutes a month

00:35:56.820 --> 00:35:57.980
uh... to call home

00:35:57.980 --> 00:35:59.100
so some guys call

00:35:59.100 --> 00:36:00.260
and minutes a day

00:36:00.260 --> 00:36:02.940
i called fifteen minutes every other day which gave me a little bit of

00:36:02.940 --> 00:36:04.180
extra time

00:36:04.180 --> 00:36:08.780
uh... every month if something came up and i needed to make a an extra call

00:36:08.780 --> 00:36:11.040
but you know twenty six cents a minute

00:36:11.040 --> 00:36:13.160
uh... it it adds up

00:36:13.160 --> 00:36:14.240
very expensive

00:36:14.240 --> 00:36:15.680
and again

00:36:15.680 --> 00:36:19.400
we've got the poorest people in society that are incarcerated they can afford to

00:36:19.400 --> 00:36:21.040
pay twenty six cents a minute

00:36:21.040 --> 00:36:26.000
yeah it's really extortion from the uh... people who are least able to uh...

00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:27.040
pay for it

00:36:27.040 --> 00:36:30.960
and it's it's a gruesome situation when you see the kickbacks

00:36:30.960 --> 00:36:35.320
that are are used to pay to lock up more people

00:36:35.320 --> 00:36:36.800
that's exactly right

00:36:36.820 --> 00:36:41.660
so uh... your wife heather lost her job at the c i a

00:36:41.660 --> 00:36:45.260
uh... just as as you were about to go to trial

00:36:45.260 --> 00:36:49.100
yeah it's actually what it was even before that uh... she was fired on the

00:36:49.100 --> 00:36:52.540
day of my arrest only because she was married to me

00:36:52.540 --> 00:36:54.060
i had been arraigned

00:36:54.060 --> 00:36:56.540
and i was in my attorney's car

00:36:56.540 --> 00:37:00.460
uh... on the way home he was going to drop me off at home

00:37:00.460 --> 00:37:04.140
after the arraignment and uh... she called on my cell phone and she said

00:37:04.140 --> 00:37:05.820
well i have bad news

00:37:05.840 --> 00:37:08.360
i got fired today and i said what

00:37:08.360 --> 00:37:09.520
what happened

00:37:09.520 --> 00:37:11.720
and she said they called me in

00:37:11.720 --> 00:37:14.600
and she was a maternity leave at the time i should add

00:37:14.600 --> 00:37:16.240
so she said they called her in

00:37:16.240 --> 00:37:18.160
and they told her

00:37:18.160 --> 00:37:19.840
that she could resign

00:37:19.840 --> 00:37:22.560
or they were going to put her through

00:37:22.560 --> 00:37:26.360
the most hellacious background investigation she could imagine

00:37:26.360 --> 00:37:27.640
and she said

00:37:27.640 --> 00:37:29.160
that's okay

00:37:29.160 --> 00:37:31.160
i don't want to work for an organization

00:37:31.160 --> 00:37:34.240
that is treated by husband the way you have treated him

00:37:34.260 --> 00:37:36.540
so she submitted her resignation

00:37:36.540 --> 00:37:37.820
right there on the spot

00:37:37.820 --> 00:37:39.500
and the funny thing is

00:37:39.500 --> 00:37:43.620
she was immediately gobbled up by a fortune one hundred company

00:37:43.620 --> 00:37:45.140
she's been promoted

00:37:45.140 --> 00:37:48.500
three times since then and she makes about triple what she was making at the

00:37:48.500 --> 00:37:49.460
cia

00:37:49.460 --> 00:37:50.580
but they're lost

00:37:50.580 --> 00:37:54.980
and now uh... former cia colleagues are reaching out to her on linkedin

00:37:54.980 --> 00:37:57.660
asking if she can get them into this company

00:37:57.660 --> 00:38:00.980
that's very interesting

00:38:01.000 --> 00:38:05.200
let me ask you a somewhat personal and probing question john

00:38:05.200 --> 00:38:06.520
and it's a long one

00:38:06.520 --> 00:38:11.800
uh... you were uh... an officer at the cia for fourteen years uh... a fairly

00:38:11.800 --> 00:38:13.600
accomplished spy

00:38:13.600 --> 00:38:18.480
and uh... you were briefed into the many of the black programs uh... of the

00:38:18.480 --> 00:38:21.920
covert operations of the intelligence community

00:38:21.920 --> 00:38:25.480
and that made you uh... what i would call a made man

00:38:25.480 --> 00:38:28.760
uh... in the the world of covert operations

00:38:29.740 --> 00:38:31.980
you were then put through the ringer

00:38:31.980 --> 00:38:35.060
uh... by that that same machinery

00:38:35.060 --> 00:38:39.820
uh... spent thirty months in prison uh... you are still on parole

00:38:39.820 --> 00:38:46.420
and uh... you know in your writings as both before during and since prison

00:38:46.420 --> 00:38:51.020
uh... you have struck a a series of positions that make you very popular

00:38:51.020 --> 00:38:53.180
with lefties like me

00:38:53.180 --> 00:38:56.060
right but but the lingering question is

00:38:56.060 --> 00:38:58.220
uh... who are you

00:38:58.280 --> 00:39:01.280
well and and the the republican party

00:39:01.280 --> 00:39:04.600
and the conservative movement in this country do not have a monopoly on

00:39:04.600 --> 00:39:05.960
patriotism

00:39:05.960 --> 00:39:07.960
uh... as far as i'm concerned it is

00:39:07.960 --> 00:39:09.720
it is we progress

00:39:09.720 --> 00:39:11.160
who are the patriots

00:39:11.160 --> 00:39:12.360
we're the ones

00:39:12.360 --> 00:39:13.720
that want to implement

00:39:13.720 --> 00:39:17.720
the plans in the programs that are founding fathers had first conceived

00:39:17.720 --> 00:39:21.480
so that you know i i would counter anybody that challenged my politics

00:39:21.480 --> 00:39:22.200
by saying that

00:39:22.200 --> 00:39:24.240
we're the patriot

00:39:24.240 --> 00:39:25.800
uh... i feel very strongly about that

00:39:25.860 --> 00:39:30.140
i come from an immigrant family all four of my grandparents came from greece

00:39:30.140 --> 00:39:32.980
uh... my my dad's father left

00:39:32.980 --> 00:39:35.940
when um... when his uh...

00:39:35.940 --> 00:39:37.700
ancestral island was occupied

00:39:37.700 --> 00:39:39.820
by the ottoman empire and then

00:39:39.820 --> 00:39:43.940
uh... the italians took it after the fall of the ottoman so uh...

00:39:43.940 --> 00:39:47.540
they they fled you know they were economic refugees basically is is what

00:39:47.540 --> 00:39:48.620
it came down to

00:39:48.620 --> 00:39:51.580
and here my grandfather tell the story

00:39:51.580 --> 00:39:55.100
franklin roosevelt was you know personally waiting for everybody at

00:39:55.100 --> 00:39:59.400
ellis island to give them passports and citizenship and jobs in the steel

00:39:59.400 --> 00:40:00.000
mill in pit

00:40:00.000 --> 00:40:07.880
And so, all my life, and all my dad's life, my grandfather talked about public service,

00:40:07.880 --> 00:40:14.320
that we owed this country so much for what it did for us that we had to pay it back.

00:40:14.320 --> 00:40:22.160
My grandfather kept a framed photo of Franklin Roosevelt on the TV until he died in 1978.

00:40:22.160 --> 00:40:24.320
I still have that picture of Franklin Roosevelt.

00:40:24.320 --> 00:40:26.400
I have it in my bedroom.

00:40:26.400 --> 00:40:29.920
So public service was very, very important.

00:40:29.920 --> 00:40:33.600
And you know, I wanted to see the world while serving the country.

00:40:33.600 --> 00:40:35.760
I had a fascination with the Middle East.

00:40:35.760 --> 00:40:38.240
I still do all my life.

00:40:38.240 --> 00:40:45.120
And I had an advisor in grad school who was actually a CIA officer undercover as a grad

00:40:45.120 --> 00:40:47.320
school advisor.

00:40:47.320 --> 00:40:52.400
And he recruited me into the CIA in 1989.

00:40:52.400 --> 00:40:58.200
So do you have a clear sense though of who you are after all that you've been through?

00:40:58.200 --> 00:41:06.280
And are you still at heart a spy who would like to return to that work and you know, deliver

00:41:06.280 --> 00:41:08.560
on missions for our country?

00:41:08.560 --> 00:41:17.400
Or do you feel that the agency that you spent 14 years in is tainted and has been assigned

00:41:17.400 --> 00:41:22.240
impossible missions and improbable missions as well?

00:41:22.240 --> 00:41:24.920
Well, yeah, the CIA is tainted.

00:41:24.920 --> 00:41:31.480
And I wouldn't and couldn't go back to the CIA because that's not the CIA that I knew.

00:41:31.480 --> 00:41:36.280
In fact, when I was at the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, I worked with a guy who

00:41:36.280 --> 00:41:38.600
had been a senior CIA officer.

00:41:38.600 --> 00:41:40.080
And we became good friends.

00:41:40.080 --> 00:41:44.880
And he said to me one time, you know, you always talk about how much you love the

00:41:44.880 --> 00:41:47.680
CIA but how the CIA has changed over the years.

00:41:47.680 --> 00:41:52.560
And certainly now it's more of a paramilitary organization than anything else.

00:41:53.000 --> 00:41:59.360
And he says, I always chuckle when I hear you say that because the CIA that you describe

00:41:59.360 --> 00:42:01.280
never really existed.

00:42:01.280 --> 00:42:02.840
It only existed in your mind.

00:42:02.840 --> 00:42:06.000
He said it's always been a troubled organization.

00:42:06.000 --> 00:42:12.680
It's always been an organization that's been antithetical to the American way of life.

00:42:12.680 --> 00:42:17.000
You had a good time, but you were so junior, he said you didn't see what was really going

00:42:17.000 --> 00:42:19.960
on upstairs at the top.

00:42:19.960 --> 00:42:23.560
And the more I think about that, the more I realize that he was probably right.

00:42:23.560 --> 00:42:29.240
I've always been a progressive, I've always been a civil libertarian at the same time.

00:42:29.240 --> 00:42:34.720
And the CIA that we have now that gladly carries out operations against American citizens

00:42:34.720 --> 00:42:39.960
even is antithetical to the American way of life.

00:42:39.960 --> 00:42:43.320
So John, let's segue now to some current issues.

00:42:43.320 --> 00:42:47.320
And I do want to highly recommend your book, Doing Time Like a Spy.

00:42:47.320 --> 00:42:54.240
It is now published and I read a pre-publication copy, but it's out there and available and

00:42:54.240 --> 00:42:58.360
I encourage people to find it and to read it.

00:42:58.360 --> 00:43:03.320
But the agency where you used to work is now embroiled in this whole Trump-Russia

00:43:03.320 --> 00:43:05.320
embryo.

00:43:05.320 --> 00:43:06.920
And I'm a skeptic.

00:43:06.920 --> 00:43:12.000
I don't believe the representations that the Director of National Intelligence offered

00:43:12.000 --> 00:43:14.680
in October or in January.

00:43:14.680 --> 00:43:18.840
The documents that they published are insulting and infantilized.

00:43:18.840 --> 00:43:27.440
We have no public evidence of the alleged Russian responsibility for hacking the DNC.

00:43:27.440 --> 00:43:35.080
And so much of what we believe to be true or people have embraced is based on anonymous

00:43:35.080 --> 00:43:40.280
information leaked by people who we can't identify.

00:43:40.280 --> 00:43:48.840
And so we have spun into this evidence-free era where a leak becomes a fact by repetition

00:43:48.840 --> 00:43:55.120
and by multiple reporting, but not by proof or multiple sources.

00:43:55.120 --> 00:44:01.320
And so I feel that we've entered this hall of mirrors that makes it very difficult

00:44:01.320 --> 00:44:06.440
for the average American to know what to believe or who to believe.

00:44:06.440 --> 00:44:12.080
Do you have any confidence in the assertions contained in the assessments from October and

00:44:12.080 --> 00:44:18.520
January about Russia's attempt to manipulate the election in favor of Trump?

00:44:18.520 --> 00:44:19.520
No.

00:44:19.520 --> 00:44:20.520
No, I don't.

00:44:20.520 --> 00:44:27.000
And I've been quite vocal about this, too, in columns that I regularly write for ReaderSupportedNews.org.

00:44:27.000 --> 00:44:28.000
No.

00:44:28.000 --> 00:44:29.000
No.

00:44:29.000 --> 00:44:36.280
Anytime the intelligence community or the FBI lays out a story and then says, take my word

00:44:36.280 --> 00:44:37.280
for it.

00:44:37.280 --> 00:44:40.560
Well, don't take their word for it.

00:44:40.560 --> 00:44:45.640
I think that this whole notion of hacking, too, is overblown because no one's ever

00:44:45.640 --> 00:44:51.400
actually defined what they mean by hacking.

00:44:51.400 --> 00:44:54.840
Did the Russians somehow steal the election from Hillary Clinton?

00:44:54.840 --> 00:44:55.840
No.

00:44:55.840 --> 00:44:57.840
Hillary Clinton was a terrible candidate.

00:44:57.840 --> 00:45:01.680
Hillary Clinton stole the primary election from Bernie Sanders.

00:45:01.680 --> 00:45:09.000
So if they're accusing the Russians of having hacked voting machines and changed votes or

00:45:09.000 --> 00:45:13.760
somehow having stolen the election for Trump, well, my God, that's a serious charge.

00:45:13.760 --> 00:45:16.040
Lay out the evidence and let's take a look at it.

00:45:16.040 --> 00:45:19.160
But they haven't laid out any evidence, and you're right.

00:45:19.160 --> 00:45:23.360
The evidence that they have provided tells us literally nothing.

00:45:23.360 --> 00:45:28.720
And with that said, and I part company with a lot of my friends on the left on this issue,

00:45:28.720 --> 00:45:31.960
I support the appointment of a special counsel.

00:45:31.960 --> 00:45:38.400
Not because I think that there's anything to these Russian issues, but because I think

00:45:38.400 --> 00:45:44.080
crimes have been committed by senior members of the Trump campaign.

00:45:44.080 --> 00:45:51.680
Mike Flynn, for example, we know failed to register as an agent for a foreign government

00:45:51.680 --> 00:45:54.160
when he was lobbying for Turkey.

00:45:54.160 --> 00:45:55.560
That is a crime.

00:45:55.560 --> 00:45:59.800
We know that he failed to register as an agent for the Russian government when he was taking

00:45:59.800 --> 00:46:01.880
money from the Russians to lobby.

00:46:01.880 --> 00:46:03.000
That is a crime.

00:46:03.000 --> 00:46:09.560
He did not report the whatever it is, $40,000 or $65,000, I've seen both figures, that

00:46:09.560 --> 00:46:12.560
he received as payment from the Russian government.

00:46:12.560 --> 00:46:14.040
That's a crime.

00:46:14.040 --> 00:46:20.600
He filled out incorrect improper information on his ethics report.

00:46:20.600 --> 00:46:22.240
That's also a crime.

00:46:22.240 --> 00:46:24.880
So that's what I'd like to get to the bottom of.

00:46:24.880 --> 00:46:29.440
Flynn, Manafort, Carter Page, possibly Roger Stone.

00:46:29.440 --> 00:46:32.360
I'd like to see what was going on inside that campaign.

00:46:32.360 --> 00:46:38.240
Getting back to the issue of hacking and the emails though, Ambassador Craig Murray,

00:46:38.240 --> 00:46:43.200
the former British ambassador to Uzbekistan who lost his job because he blew the whistle

00:46:43.200 --> 00:46:51.800
on the Uzbek torture program, said to the Guardian newspaper in London that a DNC staffer

00:46:51.800 --> 00:46:56.960
printed off the Podesta emails and gave them to Craig.

00:46:56.960 --> 00:47:01.200
And Craig personally delivered those emails to WikiLeaks.

00:47:01.200 --> 00:47:07.400
He says that there was no Russian hack, that he was the middleman between the DNC staff

00:47:07.400 --> 00:47:09.000
and WikiLeaks.

00:47:09.000 --> 00:47:11.920
But the mainstream media has ignored that assertion.

00:47:11.960 --> 00:47:19.440
Well, and the reporting, it's a little rough on the latest developments in the Seth Rich

00:47:19.440 --> 00:47:22.440
murder investigation.

00:47:22.440 --> 00:47:30.360
But it's much more credible to believe that an insider released that information than

00:47:30.360 --> 00:47:32.760
the Russian hack gave it to WikiLeaks.

00:47:32.760 --> 00:47:38.520
And your colleagues in the veteran intelligence professionals for sanity, including Bill

00:47:38.640 --> 00:47:43.680
Binney, who I've talked to on many occasions, former technical director at the NSA, they

00:47:43.680 --> 00:47:50.760
flatly maintain that if it was a hack, that the NSA could trace those packets and could

00:47:50.760 --> 00:47:57.480
determine when they were accessed, where they went, and most likely who did it.

00:47:57.480 --> 00:48:05.120
We also know that this bullshit about Fancy Bear and APT-28 and the Cyrillic reference

00:48:05.200 --> 00:48:12.000
to the founder of the KGB, anybody can create that kind of a false trail.

00:48:12.000 --> 00:48:19.440
And everybody is well aware, at least by now, that it's easy to fake your IP address.

00:48:19.440 --> 00:48:20.440
Absolutely.

00:48:20.440 --> 00:48:22.640
Hackers do it every day.

00:48:22.640 --> 00:48:23.640
Yeah.

00:48:23.640 --> 00:48:24.640
It's very easy to do.

00:48:24.640 --> 00:48:30.600
And the fact that the FBI has never examined the DNC servers, they relied on CrowdStrike

00:48:30.600 --> 00:48:35.000
to submit a report, and they just take that at face value.

00:48:35.000 --> 00:48:44.400
All of this, you know, truly undermines the, you know, much-vaunted national security apparatus

00:48:44.400 --> 00:48:45.920
that we have.

00:48:45.920 --> 00:48:53.280
But I do part company with you a bit on the selection of Mueller as the special counsel.

00:48:53.280 --> 00:48:54.280
Mainly...

00:48:54.280 --> 00:48:56.640
Well, no, no, I'm not a supporter of Mueller.

00:48:56.640 --> 00:49:00.800
It was Mueller who headed the investigation that put me in prison.

00:49:00.800 --> 00:49:05.320
It was Mueller who created what they called the John Kiriakou Task Force at the FBI.

00:49:05.320 --> 00:49:08.360
So, no, believe me, I'm no fan of Mueller.

00:49:08.360 --> 00:49:13.360
But I did think that we needed somebody as a special counsel.

00:49:13.360 --> 00:49:19.280
Well, I do think, though, that he can be relied upon to, number one, preserve the reputation

00:49:19.280 --> 00:49:20.960
of the FBI.

00:49:20.960 --> 00:49:27.000
And whatever you define as the deep state, he is a deep actor in the deep state.

00:49:27.000 --> 00:49:32.560
And I do believe that he's well positioned to dispatch Trump.

00:49:32.560 --> 00:49:33.760
But at what cost?

00:49:33.760 --> 00:49:42.840
The cost will be the public role of the deep state in running our country and picking our

00:49:42.840 --> 00:49:51.520
presidents as opposed to the murky covert role that has been exercised in the past.

00:49:51.520 --> 00:49:52.520
That's true.

00:49:52.520 --> 00:49:56.040
You know, there was an interesting editorial in the Washington Post today.

00:49:56.040 --> 00:49:59.760
And I generally hate the Washington Post, just because they...

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:03.580
uh... they're so anti whistleblower and it's not just me at any national

00:50:03.580 --> 00:50:07.560
security whistleblower but there was a fascinating editorial today in which they

00:50:07.560 --> 00:50:11.560
they laid out how trump can save himself and it was by adopting the

00:50:11.560 --> 00:50:12.860
reagan model

00:50:12.860 --> 00:50:16.320
during iran contra because it's not the crime

00:50:16.320 --> 00:50:17.600
